#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""One CPU gate for a checkout: cargo workspace tests and the script suite.

  scripts/dev/cpu-gate --task NAME [--jobs N] [--full] [--slow] [--cargo] [--scripts] [--benches] [--skip-live-planners] [--base REV] [--keep]

--jobs is the total worker budget: split evenly between Cargo and pytest when
both run, otherwise given entirely to the requested suite. Default excludes
measured full-plan/cache and live V4 tests; --full restores them and is required for
dev merges and releases. This includes the installed-example deployment-to-
release_prefill_shape sweep: HF_HOME (or ~/.cache/huggingface) is the default
inventory; CUTEAFD_DEPLOYMENT_TEST_HF_HOME overrides it. Missing checkpoints skip.
The sweep discovers PROGRAMS.json in /opt/cuteafd/share, ~/.local/share/cuteafd,
or the live V4 fixture's ~/.cache/cuteafd/builds/v3-p4/c1/artifacts (in that order).
CUTEAFD_DEPLOYMENT_TEST_PROGRAMS overrides discovery, retaining adjacent capacity
manifests. Configs requiring an absent PROGRAMS file skip with an explicit counted
reason; planner failures with present inputs still fail.
Receipts name every exclusion. --slow separately
adds the explicit ignored CPU soaks and is also required at release cuts. Install cargo-nextest
without root into the persistent architecture Cargo home (see AGENTS.md).
The gate finds it on PATH or there automatically; cargo test remains the fallback.
When cargo or rustc is not on PATH (a systemd user unit), the gate prepends the
existing standard Cargo bin directories ($CARGO_HOME/bin, ~/.cargo/bin, the
persistent architecture Cargo home, Homebrew's bin, and the toolchain rustup
resolves when /opt/rustup exists) for itself and every child before refusing.

With none of --cargo, --scripts, --benches the first two run. --benches runs
the release host-cost bounds (BENCHES: the draft policy's select median at the
V4.1 C16 geometry under CUTEAFD_POLICY_BENCH_MAX_US) as their own suite,
recording each bench's medians. Everything lives under
~/.cache/cuteafd/builds/NAME (asserted build filesystem): the Cargo target
(target-cpu-gate, plain rustc, kept apart from kache targets), TMPDIR and
pytest's basetemp. Submodules are initialized at their pinned commits first.
Compiling (cargo test --no-run) holds a build slot (scripts/build/build-slot.sh,
slot 0 is build.lock); running the tests holds none. Each suite has its own
timeout. The python is the checkout's .venv, else the main checkout's.

The verdict comes from the parsed test summaries, never from an exit code
alone: a suite passes only when its summaries report more than zero tests and
no failures. Failing test ids are listed per suite. --base REV runs the same
gate on a detached worktree of REV and reports only the ids that are new in
this checkout. --skip-live-planners skips the slow live-inventory V4 planner
tests (real checkpoints + manifests) and records that choice. Unlike the optional
installed-example sweep, V4 live tests fail when their required inputs are missing.
Their compiler provenance must match the checkout's SparkInfer pin.

Writes NAME/cpu-gate.json and NAME/cpu-gate.md; exit 0 only when every
requested suite passed (or, with --base, introduced no new failing ids).
Both list the largest scratch consumers (bytes allocated under TMPDIR when the
suites end, grouped by pytest test or by top-level entry). The scratch (tmp/)
and the Cargo targets (tens of GB per run) are deleted on every exit, failure
and interrupt included; --keep leaves them for inspection. Logs and receipts
stay.
"""
